  (:adr/context
  "Phase F (final phase) of the eKYC/AML vendor plan approved earlier this
   session, closing the loop opened by ADR-2607198200 (kotoba-lang/mrz,
   Phase A) through ADR-2607199100 (kotoba-lang/ekyc-native-provider,
   Phase E). This actor is the customer-facing consumer of all five prior
   phases: real ICAO 9303 document decode (mrz), real OFAC/UN sanctions
   screening (watchlist-screen), real EAR/MAR/yaw liveness challenge
   scoring (face-liveness), a deliberate no-algorithm face-match stub
   (face-match), and the composing ekyc.adapters.provider implementation
   wiring the first three together (ekyc-native-provider).

   Registry note (ISIC code selection): the natural 3-digit group code
   '649' (\"Other financial service activities, except insurance and
   pension funding activities\") existed as an unpromoted `:spec`
   placeholder (`:repo nil :business-id nil`) with a `:superseded-by
   [\"6491\" \"6492\" \"6493\" \"6499\"]` marker noting its 4-digit
   children. All four of those children, and every other semantically
   closer identity-verification/credit-bureau-adjacent code checked this
   session (6619 Card Transaction Processing, 6399 Meta Job Search, 8010
   Private security, 8020 Security systems, 8030 Investigation
   activities, 8219, 8220, 8291 Corporate/Compliance Intelligence, 8299),
   were already claimed by unrelated implemented cloud-itonami businesses
   as of this session (this registry is a near-complete, extremely
   actively-edited fleet — re-verified live via GitHub API immediately
   before promotion, not assumed from a local checkout, given the
   observed high concurrent-edit frequency on this exact file this
   session). '649' is promoted directly with a custom `:name` (same
   precedent `cloud-itonami-isic-8291` itself used — deviating from a
   literal ISIC title when the business's actual scope is more specific),
   its `:superseded-by` marker removed as now-inconsistent with an
   `:implemented` business occupying the code, and its stale
   `:operating-states` (a generic `[:intake :onboard :transact :clear
   :settle :audit]` template placeholder) corrected to this actor's real
   StateGraph node sequence. Flagged here, as `cloud-itonami-isic-8292`'s
   own ADR flagged a similar imperfect-fit situation, for registry
   maintainers to potentially reassign to a cleaner codepoint later.")
  (:adr/decision
  "Real langgraph-clj StateGraph (`intake → verify → govern → decide →
   {commit | request-approval → commit | hold}`), architecturally
   identical in shape to `cloud-itonami-isic-8291`'s OperationActor except
   `:verify` replaces `:advise`: a deterministic fan-out to the real
   engines above (`kyc-actor.verifier/real-verifier`), NOT an LLM
   proposal — a deliberate, ADR-documented (`docs/adr/0001-architecture.md`
   in the actor repo itself) departure from every other actor in this
   fleet's Advisor-LLM ⊣ Governor template, reasoned as: an identity-
   verification VERDICT should come from checksum/geometry/matching math
   with a real testable answer, not language generation, which would
   introduce a hallucination-shaped failure mode into exactly the domain
   this whole multi-phase effort was structured to keep honest.

   VerificationGovernor (`kyc-actor.policy`), 3 HARD (un-overridable) + 3
   SOFT (always-escalate) checks, mirroring DisclosureGovernor's 3+3
   shape:
   1. sanctions-hard-gate — a watchlist hit at :exact confidence never
      auto-approves.
   2. liveness-required-gate — approving a case without a completed,
      :verified liveness check is a HARD block.
   3. accreditation-claim-gate — this actor's own honesty backstop
      (unique among this fleet's Governors): any proposal/response text
      claiming regulatory accreditation this self-built service does not
      hold is blocked structurally, server-side. Doubled at the API layer
      (`kyc-actor.api`'s enforced `disclosures` block on every response)
      so a single point of enforcement isn't one bug from a silent
      regression.
   4. confidence-floor / 5. pep-flag-gate (no PEP data source wired --
      always false today, honestly, not guessed) / 6.
      correction-request-always-escalates.

   Real CACAO/did:key self-mint identity (`kyc-actor.identity`), ported
   from `gftdcojp/cloud-itonami`'s `identity.clj` (ADR-2607141700),
   streamlined (no multi-audience CLI dispatch, single kotobase.net
   target) but functionally faithful: real Ed25519 seed generation, real
   `did:key` derivation via `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-ed25519`, real CACAO
   minting via `kotoba-lang/org-chainagnostic-cacao`, atomic
   create-if-absent persistence via `Files/write`'s `CREATE_NEW` — tested
   end-to-end, not merely copied.

   `docs/legal/data-handling.md` (mirroring `network-isekai`'s
   `legal/terms.md` `[CONFIRM: ...]` convention, per this session's
   earlier Phase A research finding that pattern already established in
   this workspace) documents six explicit unresolved legal/compliance
   questions this repo does not resolve — retention period, legal basis
   for processing, minor-protection law applicability, sanctions-
   screening notice obligations, `:hold` liability, and a real Privacy
   Policy/ToS — none silently assumed answered.

   38 tests / 90 assertions covering governor hard/soft checks (positive
   AND negative per gate), phase-gate autonomy levels, store lifecycle,
   real `langgraph-clj` interrupt-before/resume human-in-the-loop, the
   verifier's real engine integration against the same ICAO Doc 9303 TD3
   fixture `kotoba-lang/mrz` itself uses plus a real blink-frame sequence,
   `kyc-actor.api`'s enforced disclosures, and the CACAO identity module's
   real key-generation/minting/persistence. `clj-kondo`: 0 errors, 0
   warnings. `clojure -M:dev:run` demo verified working end-to-end
   (clean-auto-commit / sanctioned-hard-hold / correction-escalate-
   approve scenarios).")
  (:adr/consequences
  (vector "This actor is the working, tested proof that the five prior Phase A-E repos (kotoba-lang/mrz, watchlist-screen, face-liveness, face-match, ekyc-native-provider) genuinely compose into an end-to-end governed verification flow -- not five isolated libraries that were never actually wired together."
   "Every honest capability gap from every upstream engine passes through unchanged and undisguised: this actor's own MATURITY.md enumerates them again at the consuming layer rather than letting a reader assume they were quietly closed by 'the final integration.'"
   "The accreditation-claim-gate + disclosures-block double enforcement means no code path in this actor can represent itself as a licensed/accredited KYC-AML provider -- a structural guarantee, not a documentation promise, for a domain (identity fraud, AML) where that distinction has real consequences if violated."
   "Not deployed anywhere; no live kotobase.net session exercised end-to-end; MemStore only (state lost on restart); no HTTP transport wired -- all explicit, documented follow-ups (MATURITY.md), not silently deferred."
   "ISIC '649' is registered against this actor's specific identity-verification scope, not the code's literal ISIC title -- flagged for registry maintainer review, following cloud-itonami-isic-8292's own precedent for the same kind of imperfect-fit situation."))
  (:adr/references (vector 2607198200 2607198500 2607198700 2607198900 2607199100))))
